arbiter_feedback.description:
The regex `[^\S
]+` on a line that was produced by splitting on `
` can never contain `
`, so the `
`-exclusion in the character class is redundant but harmless. However, the `.strip()` call strips ALL Unicode whitespace (including `	`, `\x0b`, `\x0c`, `\xa0`, etc.), while the regex only targets `[^\S
]`. These two operations are slightly inconsistent: `.strip()` removes leading/trailing non-newline whitespace including exotic Unicode whitespace that `[^\S
]+` might not have collapsed if it appeared at the boundary alone. This is benign in practice but indicates the logic is not fully intentional.
